{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0371"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge nature/plants","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/nature__plants.g1.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",54],["-","Five"],["+","Six"],["=",629],["+","A cousin of the banana makes the cup of tea possible. Abacá, Musa textilis, is a fibre banana from the Philippines whose leaf sheaths yield a long, strong and salt-resistant fibre, traded for centuries as Manila hemp for ships' ropes. Because it stays strong when wet, it is today the main raw material of tea-bag paper: when Teekanne launched Adolf Rambold's double-chamber tea bag in 1949, it was made largely of abacá fibre.\n\n"],["=",320],["+","tea-bag paper | leaf-sheath fibres | abacá |\n| "],["=",87]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",70],["-","Fünf"],["+","Sechs"],["=",581],["+","Eine Verwandte der Banane macht die Tasse Tee möglich. Abacá, Musa textilis, ist eine Faserbanane von den Philippinen, deren Blattscheiden eine lange, reißfeste und salzwasserbeständige Faser liefern, die jahrhundertelang als Manilahanf für Schiffstaue gehandelt wurde. Weil sie auch nass fest bleibt, ist sie heute der wichtigste Rohstoff für Teebeutelpapier: Als Teekanne 1949 Adolf Rambolds Doppelkammerbeutel auf den Markt brachte, bestand er großteils aus Abacá-Fasern.\n\n"],["=",292],["+","Teebeutelpapier | Blattscheidenfasern | Abacá |\n| "],["=",73]]},{"field":"notesEn","old":"","new":"Also accepted: Manila hemp, fibre banana (Musa textilis)."},{"field":"notesDe","old":"","new":"Auch richtig: Manilahanf, Faserbanane (Musa textilis)."}]},
